Step 1 — Five Stress Tests
Stress Test 1: Interest Drift

My Scenario: I could get further into my cybersecurity degree or start working in the field and realize that I do not enjoy it as much as I thought I would. I may also find another area of technology or business that interests me more.

Impact on My Roadmap: This would affect almost my entire roadmap because most of my classes, certifications, internships, and career goals are focused on cybersecurity.

Vulnerability Rating: 3 / 5

Mitigation Strategy: I can explore different areas of cybersecurity early, talk with people already working in the field, and get internship experience before fully committing to one specific cybersecurity career.

Stress Test 2: Market Disruption

My Scenario: Cybersecurity could change significantly over the next few years because of AI, automation, new technology, or changes in what employers are looking for.

Impact on My Roadmap: This would mainly affect my skills, certifications, and job search because the skills employers want when I graduate could be different from what they want today.

Vulnerability Rating: 3 / 5

Mitigation Strategy: I need to stay updated on changes in cybersecurity instead of only relying on what I learn in school. I can continue learning new tools, earning relevant certifications, and adjusting my skills based on what employers are looking for.

Stress Test 3: Academic Obstacles

My Scenario: I could struggle with a difficult cybersecurity, programming, or technical course. Failing or having to retake a required class could delay other courses or possibly my graduation.

Impact on My Roadmap: This would mostly affect my academic lane, but it could also affect internships and when I am ready to start applying for full-time positions.

Vulnerability Rating: 4 / 5

Mitigation Strategy: Instead of waiting until I am struggling, I can use tutoring, office hours, study groups, and other resources at UT. I also need to manage my time better and give myself more time for difficult technical courses.

Stress Test 4: Life Interruptions

My Scenario: An unexpected personal, financial, family, or other major life situation could make it difficult for me to continue school at the pace I originally planned.

Impact on My Roadmap: This could affect every lane because slowing down school could also delay internships, certifications, networking, and graduation.

Vulnerability Rating: 4 / 5

Mitigation Strategy: I can make my roadmap flexible instead of assuming everything has to happen within exactly four years. I can also plan ahead financially, communicate with my advisor when problems happen, and be willing to adjust my course load instead of giving up on the overall goal.

Stress Test 5: Competitive Reality

My Scenario: I could graduate and realize that having a cybersecurity degree alone does not make me stand out enough for the jobs I want. I may be competing against people who already have certifications, internships, projects, and more technical experience.

Impact on My Roadmap: This would have the biggest impact during my junior and senior years when I begin applying for internships and full-time cybersecurity jobs.

Vulnerability Rating: 5 / 5

Mitigation Strategy: I need to start building experience before graduation instead of waiting until senior year. I want to complete internships, work on cybersecurity projects, earn certifications like Security+, build my professional network, and use my previous Army experience and leadership skills as part of my professional background.

Step 2 — My Three Biggest Vulnerabilities

1. Competitive Reality: I think this is my biggest vulnerability because getting a degree does not automatically guarantee me a cybersecurity job. I need to make sure I graduate with actual experience, certifications, projects, and connections instead of only having a degree.

2. Academic Obstacles: Cybersecurity has technical subjects that I may struggle with, especially as the courses become more advanced. If I fall behind in an important course, it could affect my graduation timeline, so I need to be willing to ask for help early.

3. Life Interruptions: I know from my own experience that life does not always follow the plan you make. An unexpected situation could slow down my degree, but I think having a flexible timeline would allow me to adjust without completely changing my career goal.

Step 3 — Stress-Test Reflection

Before this stress-test, I assumed that as long as I finished my cybersecurity degree, I would be in a good position to start my career. After examining my plan more carefully, I now realize that graduating is only one part of what I need to do. I will also be competing with people who have internships, certifications, projects, and other experience before they graduate.

The most important change I need to make to my roadmap is to focus more on getting real experience while I am still in school. Instead of waiting until my senior year to worry about getting a job, I need to start building my resume and professional network early. I also realized that I need to keep my plan flexible because cybersecurity can change and unexpected things can happen in my own life.

Running this analysis has changed the way I think about planning because I now see that a good plan should not depend on everything going perfectly. I need backup options and the ability to adjust while still working toward my main goal.
